"""Render the lateral filter study from measured results."""
import json,sys,html
from pathlib import Path
root=Path(sys.argv[1]);rows=json.loads((root/'summary.json').read_text());checks=json.loads((root/'nonlinear-summary.json').read_text())
labels=['LPF 4 Hz','LPF 1 Hz','Notch 2.5 Hz z=0.2']
report='''<!doctype html><meta charset="utf-8"><title>Lateral frequency shaping</title><style>body{font:17px/1.55 system-ui;max-width:1150px;margin:32px auto;padding:0 20px;color:#18333e}img{width:100%}table{border-collapse:collapse;font-size:15px}td,th{padding:9px;border-bottom:1px solid #ccc;text-align:left}pre{white-space:pre-wrap;background:#eef3f6;padding:18px}</style><h1>Improve lateral robustness without increasing the gains</h1><p>10 October 2026 · high-DOF mission model · Trout Lake 1.758 kg · fixed throttle at each trim</p>
<p><strong>Recommend a 1 Hz measurement filter for the next native SITL trial, keeping all gains unchanged.</strong> A broad 2.5 Hz notch is also promising and generally gives stronger local margins, but a simple cutoff change delivers most of the practical benefit across both turn directions. Neither candidate has been installed in the flight controller by this study.</p>
<h2>Experiment</h2><p>Compared six filters at five relative equilibria: level, climb +0.5 m/s, descent −0.5 m/s, and level turns at ±10 deg/s. Each plant is relinearized from the nonlinear model. Heading and roll measurement channels share the same filter. P=.005, I=.002, roll damping=.003 and feedforward=.009 remain fixed. Speed scaling is frozen at each equilibrium for linear analysis and recomputed during nonlinear tests. The finite-difference perturbation uses the active brake branch and its actual configured PWM span (1300 μs left, 1500 μs right).</p>
<pre>e = r − H(s) ψ̇
u = S K_FF r + (S K_P + K_I/s)e − S K_RD H(s)p

Baseline: H₄(s) = 25.133 / (s + 25.133)
Simple candidate: H₁(s) = 6.283 / (s + 6.283)
Notch candidate: H_N(s) = H₄(s) × (s² + 6.283s + 246.740) / (s² + 21.991s + 246.740)</pre>
<p>The notch has unity DC gain and approximately −10.9 dB attenuation at 2.5 Hz relative to the baseline filter. Feedforward bypasses these measurement filters, preserving the initial commanded response. This is feedback frequency shaping, not a change to the physical model.</p>
<h2>Gain margins across operating points</h2><table><tr><th>Trim</th><th>Filter</th><th>Gain margin dB</th><th>Peak sensitivity</th><th>Step absolute-error integral</th></tr>'''
for r in rows:
    if r['controller'] in labels:report+=f"<tr><td>{r['trim']}</td><td>{r['controller']}</td><td>{r['gain_margin_db']:.2f}</td><td>{r['peak_sensitivity']:.2f}</td><td>{r['iae']:.3f} deg</td></tr>"
report+='''</table><p>All 30 no-delay linear closed loops are stable. Margins scale all feedback gains together; they do not scale feedforward. The step is +0.2 deg/s and the error integral spans 45 seconds. Similar error integrals show that the robustness improvement does not cost much local tracking performance. Climb overshoot remains substantial: this filter experiment does not fix every aspect of the controller.</p>
<img src="shaping-bode.png" alt="Loop magnitude and sensitivity comparison"><img src="shaping-step.png" alt="Local command response comparison"><img src="trim-margins.png" alt="Gain and common delay margins across five trims">
<h2>Delay interpretation</h2><p>For each filter, all unity-gain crossings are located and the smallest positive common pure-sensing-delay crossing is calculated. Baseline first boundaries are about 43–68 ms in level, climb, descent and right-turn cases; left turn is much less sensitive to this particular delay. Candidate filters remove the high-frequency unity-gain crossings, leaving a low-frequency crossing and mathematical delay limits of several seconds. <strong>Those large values are not an acceptable avionics latency specification.</strong> They are conditional stability boundaries for this fixed local plant, common delay on both measurement channels, and otherwise ideal feedback. Different delays, model uncertainty, actuator lag, navigation and altitude loops need their own checks.</p>
<h2>Nonlinear check</h2><p>Applied ±2 deg/s command increments from level and ±10 deg/s turns, at 400 Hz, with zero or 80 ms common sensing delay. Runs last 30 seconds, with native actuator travel limits, brake asymmetry, changing speed scale and integral clamping/anti-windup. Filters start at the equilibrium measurement, and the integrator is initialized to the equilibrium command. Low-pass filters use the native backward-Euler discretization; the proposed notch uses a bilinear discretization. These are simulator truth measurements with controlled delay, not an EKF or native SITL run.</p><table><tr><th>Trim</th><th>Filter</th><th>Delay ms</th><th>Last 10 s rate RMSE deg/s</th><th>30 s absolute-error integral deg</th></tr>'''
for r in checks:report+=f"<tr><td>{r['trim']}</td><td>{r['controller']}</td><td>{r['delay_ms']:.0f}</td><td>{r['last10_rmse']:.3f}</td><td>{r['iae']:.3f}</td></tr>"
report+='''</table><img src="nonlinear-shaping.png" alt="Nonlinear tracking and control effort with and without delay"><p>The current filter develops oscillation with 80 ms delay in the level and right-turn cases. Both candidates retain tracking in these finite-duration checks. The left-turn baseline also remains well behaved, consistent with its different linear delay boundary.</p>
<h2>Decision and next experiment</h2><p>Carry the 1 Hz cutoff into a native SITL comparison with the existing autotest: baseline versus cutoff-only change, retaining the existing gains. Check mission tracking, turbulence recovery, actuator activity and logged estimator delay. Keep the notch as an alternative if the simple filter loses useful disturbance rejection or a modal peak persists. This study supports trying the filter change; it does not establish flight readiness.</p>
<h2>Reproduction and provenance</h2><p>Model revision af6b2ca963. Scripts in the tinkering repository: research/archive/legacy-tools/shape_lateral.py and research/archive/legacy-tools/report_lateral_shaping.py; the experiment reuses the coordinate chart and linearization functions in research/archive/legacy-tools/classical_lateral.py. The original classical study is the source of the level trim. Cached trims should be removed after changing model configuration. All raw traces stay in Mission Planner; only final figures and scripts are archived in tinkering.</p><pre>python3 research/archive/legacy-tools/shape_lateral.py --model PATH/Tools/simulation/paraglider --source PATH/classical-lateral --output PATH/lateral-shaping
python3 research/archive/legacy-tools/report_lateral_shaping.py PATH/lateral-shaping</pre><p><a href="summary.json">Linear metrics</a> · <a href="nonlinear-summary.json">Nonlinear metrics</a> · <a href="../classical-lateral/report.html">Original classical analysis</a></p>'''
(root/'report.html').write_text(report)